{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 9, :comments ["<b>And YHWH said to Cain: Where is Abel your brother?</b> A great wonder regarding Cain — how could he say, ״I did not know״? If he was mistaken in this, to say that YHWH does not see and does not understand the deeds of those below — if so, why did he bring a meal-offering to YHWH? And it appears to say that Cain certainly understood that the Holy One, blessed be He, was questioning him about the killing of his brother, and he answered, ״I did not know; am I my brother's keeper?״ — meaning, I did not know that there is any sin in the matter of killing, and ״am I my brother's keeper״ is not an expression of astonishment but is attached to ״I did not know,״ for he said: I did not know whether I am obligated to guard my brother's head lest he be killed by my hand, and this guarding is in the language of ״אַךְ אֶת נַפְשׁוֹ שְׁמֹר״ (Job 2:6). Thus Cain claimed that he did not know whether he was obligated to guard his brother's head or not. And the Holy One, blessed be He, said to him: How could you not know, when this commandment is among those graspable by reason! And this is what He said: ״The voice of your brother's bloods cries out to Me from the ground״ — that reason obligates the fulfillment of this commandment even if you were not commanded concerning it, for in its nullification each man would swallow up his fellow alive, and the land would become a desolation without inhabitant (Isaiah 6:11). If so, the land demands vengeance for the slain, for the endurance of the land depends on this: for if there were license to kill, one's brother, then it is impossible for the land to endure, for its existence would be nullified and it would be desolate without inhabitant. Therefore ״it will not continue to give you its strength.״ And the reason for this: just as the land is affected by the matter and seeks justice for the killing, so too the land itself is obligated to execute justice against murderers, in the manner stated (Numbers 35:33): ״For blood — it flatters the land, and the land cannot be atoned for the blood that is shed upon it except by the blood of him who shed it.״ And one should ponder in what sense the blood flatters the land." "And what seems close to me to say on this is that the earth is always as though it has opened its mouth to swallow everything, as stated (Proverbs 30:15–16): ״There are three things that are never satisfied: Sheol and the barred womb,״ etc. And as stated (Isaiah 5:14): ״Therefore Sheol has enlarged herself, and opened her mouth without measure,״ etc. And from all this is a proof that the mouth of the earth is always open to swallow, and one who gives something into its mouth is as though flattering it — and this is said of the murderers who shed innocent blood, for with this blood they flatter the earth, giving it something in its mouth to swallow. And correspondingly, the earth also flatters them, giving to those who are unworthy all their needs that come from the earth, that is, two things: first, every food that is eaten and the rest of human needs that come from the earth; second, the dwelling and rest that those who dwell upon it have. Therefore, for all generations the land cannot be atoned except by the blood of him who shed it, lest they say that the two of them flatter each other — for the wicked give something into its mouth to swallow, and the earth flatters them by giving them their full needs, and the land requires atonement for this slight benefit that the murderer received from the earth before he killed, for it was not just that it gave him its strength. But here, for the sake of the settlement of the world, Cain was not killed; if so, how does the earth escape the suspicion, lest they say that the two of them flatter each other, and why does it not require atonement? Rather, because it fulfilled the will of its Creator, who said, ״It will not continue to give you its strength; a fugitive and a wanderer shall you be in the earth.״ For from the time you did this deed, the earth comes with authorization and demands of you your brother's blood, and the voice of his bloods cries out to Me from the earth, and it too will execute justice upon you, that you shall no longer receive from it the two benefits that all who dwell upon it have: namely, all needs — concerning this He said, ״It will not continue to give you its strength״; and dwelling and rest — concerning this He said, ״A fugitive and a wanderer shall you be in the earth,״ and no place at all will wish to receive you. And through this the earth escapes the suspicion of flattery, for it will not flatter you, and therefore it does not require atonement either; but to turn back and kill you now is impossible for the sake of the settlement of the world, for man had no other son at that time."], :book :genesis, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 4, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:27:11.167040222Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}